From: Hedi Chehaidar <go69zan@mytum.de>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 16:19:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix: decide escapes by the parity of the 255 run, not by
 one byte

Two places decide whether a byte is a code or the escaped literal of an
escape pair by looking at the single byte before it:

- the suffix automaton's pseudo-end states, which go to error when the byte
  before the match's first code is 255;
- the middle-start scan loops emitted by the C++ and LLVM code generators,
  which skip a candidate byte when the byte before it is 255 (the scalar
  loop's prevByte, the SIMD candidate check, and the prevByte seeded from
  data[strIdx - 1] on re-entry).

One byte cannot settle it: 255 is the escape marker unless it is itself the
escaped 255 literal of the pair before it. Every non-255 byte ends a token,
so what decides it is the parity of the run of 255 bytes in front of the
byte: an even run leaves it a code, an odd run escapes it.

Rows that contain a raw 0xFF (escaped as 255 255) therefore produced false
negatives: "x\xFFy" LIKE '%y' on every backend, and "\xFF\xFF\xFFthe" LIKE
'%he%' on the code-generated backends, which re-enter the scan with the
literal 255 as prevByte.

Suffix side: each pseudo-end now alternates with an "odd" state on 255
(even run: accept as before, odd run: error) instead of failing on the
first 255. Forward side: the generated scan loops track whether they stand
on a literal, seeded by walking back over the 255 run (isEscapedLiteral),
and the SIMD candidate check uses the same helper. The interpreter's forward
path already handled escapes through the sink state and is unchanged.

